# P110 — Cyclic shift–join partition dynamics

Status: **FINAL MECHANICAL QA PASS / GO INTERNAL / EXTERNAL HOLD**.

For each `n >= 1`, the phase space is the partition lattice `Pi_n` of the
cyclic group `C_n = Z/nZ`.  If `rho(i)=i+1`, the update is

```text
J_n(pi) = pi join rho(pi).
```

The draft proves the following exact package.

1. `J_n^t(pi)` is the join of the consecutive translates
   `pi,rho(pi),...,rho^t(pi)`.
2. The endpoint is the coset partition of the subgroup generated by all
   differences of elements initially lying in one block.
3. Recurrent points are precisely the `tau(n)` cyclic coset partitions, and
   `zeta=(1-z)^(-tau(n))`.
4. The basin whose endpoint subgroup has order `h|n` has size
   `sum_{d|h} mu(h/d) Bell(d)^(n/d)`.
5. For `n>=3`, the exact maximum depth is `n-2`.  Its deepest states are
   exactly the atoms with one block `{a,a+d}`, `gcd(d,n)=1`, so the deepest
   shell has size `n*phi(n)/2`.

The deepest-shell classification is proved by a binary cut-defect argument;
it is not inferred from the exact probe.  The manuscript presents two
separate analytic routes: lattice automorphisms plus cyclic subgroups for the
endpoint/basin package, and translated chord graphs plus cut defects for the
sharp-time package.

Run the deterministic exact control with:

```bash
python3 code/verify.py
```

The fresh output is stored in `CONTROL_OUTPUT.txt`.  It records **1,916,206**
exact assertions over all **142,417** partitions through `n=10`, every
nonconstant binary cut through `n=12`, closed basin identities through
`n=50`, and temporal Möbius/zeta checks through period 60.

Build the PDF with the four-stage commands in [BUILD.md](BUILD.md).

## Owner-subtracted boundary

- Stanley owns standard partition-lattice, Bell-number, and enumerative
  background.
- Anagnostopoulou-Merkouri–Bailey–Cameron own current structural work on
  invariant partition lattices and permutation block systems.
- Britnell–Wildon own orbit-coherence questions for joins and meets of orbit
  partitions.
- Artin–Mazur own the periodic-point zeta construction.

The least invariant coarsening obtained by joining group translates, the
cyclic subgroup/coset description of invariant partitions, and the
join/generated-subgroup correspondence are treated as background mechanisms,
not standalone contributions.  The residual scope is only the conjunction of
this temporal update with the displayed Möbius–Bell basin profile and the
sharp transient/deepest-shell theorem.

A bounded search did not locate the same package, but search absence is not a
novelty or priority certificate.  Public release, submission, and specialist
contact remain **HOLD**.

## Internal collision firewall

P97 iterates nonlinear sumset squaring `A -> A+A` on nonempty subsets of a
prime cyclic group, while P105 prunes the least label from every nontrivial
cycle of a permutation.  Within P107--P111, the other four systems act on
residue-ring ideals, a capped integer square, finite-field subspaces, and
random Heisenberg products.  P110 instead acts on the full set-partition
lattice by joining a partition with its cyclic relabelling.  Shared cyclic
labels, Bell numbers, Möbius inversion, transient clocks, or zeta bookkeeping
receive no contribution credit; the phase spaces and update rules are not
parameter variants.

The two independent team-internal reviews are recorded in
[`HOSTILE_REVIEW_A.md`](HOSTILE_REVIEW_A.md) and
[`HOSTILE_REVIEW_B.md`](HOSTILE_REVIEW_B.md).  They are not external referee
reports.  Their consolidated decision is in
[`HOSTILE_REVIEW.md`](HOSTILE_REVIEW.md); the deterministic artifact freeze
is recorded in [`FINAL_QA.md`](FINAL_QA.md) and `SHA256SUMS`.  No external
posting, submission, specialist contact, novelty, or priority action is
authorized.
# P169 — Successor Transfer on Canonically Ordered Set Partitions

**Status:** `ROUND2 DUAL-REVIEW FREEZE / GREEN_OWNER_THIN / HOLD_EXTERNAL`  
**Format:** anonymous `amsart` 10pt short theory note  
**Final repaired PDF:** 5 A4 pages, 392,380 bytes

## Result

On a canonically ordered set partition, every nonsingleton block
simultaneously sends its maximum to the cyclic successor block.  The frozen
theorem package gives:

- the exact restricted-growth update: increment the final occurrence of each
  repeated letter modulo the block count;
- sharp stratum clock `min(n-2,2k-2)` for `1<k<n`, and global clock `n-2`;
- complete dense/sparse recurrent forms, counts, and exact nontrivial period
  `k`;
- an explicit five-state matrix product for the fibre of every target and an
  exact image test;
- the interlacing witness `025|134` versus `035|124`, with equal ordered
  size/minimum/maximum data but fibres two and one;
- explicit handling of `n=1`, `k=1`, `k=n`, `n=2k`, singleton blocks, and the
  cyclic wrap.

The theorem is deliberately owner-thin.  Restricted-growth encodings and
whirling, directed-cycle chip firing, Bulgarian solitaire, promotion/jeu de
taquin/rowmotion, box-ball systems, set-partition stack sorting, Stirling
counts, and generic transfer matrices are background.  The bounded source
search grants no external lifecycle permission.

